Stored credential connectivity checks: IPv6 literals and mapped IPv4
==================================================================

Previously a bare IPv6 address produced an invalid unbracketed HTTP URL, a
bracketed address failed hostname resolution, and IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses
were checked inconsistently against the private-target policy. Valid private
management targets could therefore fail before a useful connectivity check.

The endpoint now parses bracketed targets strictly as IPv6 literals and
normalizes IP literals before the existing security checks. IPv4-mapped IPv6
uses its embedded IPv4 address for validation and transport. Metadata, loopback
and public-address checks all run on that canonical target; mapping is not an
exception to those checks. A bracketed hostname or bracketed IPv4 address is
rejected. Hostnames continue through the existing validation and pinning path.

Pinned native IPv6 addresses are bracketed when constructing the HTTP URL.
IPv4 URLs retain their previous form. Hostname SNI/Host handling, private CA
trust, certificate verification, proxy isolation, header-only streaming and
the shared transport deadline are preserved. No redirects are followed, and
plaintext fallback still requires explicit opt-in and never follows a completed
HTTPS status. A 2xx result means connectivity, not verified authentication.

Fifty API/PostgreSQL policy regressions reproduce twenty-four old failures and
twenty-six controls. These tests deliberately use an outgoing-client double
with HTTPX's real URL parser; they are not transport or TLS evidence. They cover
bare/bracketed unique-local IPv6, bare/bracketed IPv4-mapped IPv6, existing IPv4,
HTTP statuses, fallback settings, permission/tenant/site denials, native/mapped
metadata and public targets, mapped loopback, and invalid bracketed forms.

A separate before/after probe uses actual PostgreSQL, incoming TCP HTTP and
owned outgoing IPv6/IPv4 TLS sockets without HTTPX, resolver or security doubles.
It adds untrusted-CA and explicit TLS-opt-out controls for each new literal form.
The peer verifies the destination address, Host header, absence of DNS SNI on
literal requests and receipt of only synthetic Basic Authentication. Each API
request checks all 51 existing credential rows. Local qualification uses an
owned ULA and private IPv4 inside a network namespace. Container qualification
requires an owned internal IPv6-enabled network with inspected IPv4/IPv6
addresses and its own source-bound results; local success does not imply that
container qualification has completed.

Only the credential-test function changes. No database schema, historical rows,
frontend, OpenAPI or existing test files are changed. IPv6 zone identifiers
remain rejected by the existing input schema. General adapter/discovery IPv6,
hostname-to-IPv6 DNS behavior, adversarial DNS rebinding, concurrent authority
revocation and vendor authentication are not qualified by this correction.
No customer devices are contacted and no enterprise-readiness claim is made.
